> [!MARKS] 3 + 7 = 10 Marks
> **a) Structure vs Union (`3m`):** Side-by-side comparison across storage, size, active members, and initialization | **b) `Student` Structure-Array Program (`7m`):** Complete C program reading `n` records (`roll`, `name`, `marks[3]`) and printing an aligned table with averages.

### a) Structure vs Union in C (3 Marks)

> [!COMPARE] Structure (`struct`) vs Union (`union`) in C
> | Parameter | Structure (`struct`) | Union (`union`) |
> |---|---|---|
> | **Keyword** | Declared with `struct`. | Declared with `union`. |
> | **Memory Allocation** | Every member receives its **own separate memory offset**. | All members **share the same memory block** starting at offset `0`. |
> | **Total `sizeof`** | `>=` **sum** of all member sizes (plus alignment padding). | Equal to the size of the **largest single member** (plus alignment). |
> | **Simultaneous Values** | **All members** hold independent valid values simultaneously. | **Only one member** holds a meaningful value at any instant. |
> | **Mutation Side Effect** | Updating one member never alters other members. | Writing to one member overwrites the shared bytes of all others. |
> | **Initialization** | All members can be initialized together: `{101, "Riya", {80, 85, 90}}`. | Standard C89 initializes only the first member (or one designated member in C99). |

### b) C Program for `Student` Structure Array & Tabular Display (7 Marks)

```c
#include <stdio.h>

struct Student {
    int roll;
    char name[50];
    float marks[3];
};

int main(void) {
    int n;
    struct Student s[100];

    printf("Enter number of students (1-100): ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n <= 0 || n > 100) {
        printf("Invalid student count.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("\n--- Student %d ---\n", i + 1);
        printf("Roll number: ");
        scanf("%d", &s[i].roll);
        printf("Name: ");
        scanf(" %49[^\n]", s[i].name);
        printf("Marks in 3 subjects: ");
        scanf("%f %f %f", &s[i].marks[0], &s[i].marks[1], &s[i].marks[2]);
    }

    printf("\n%-8s %-20s %8s %8s %8s %10s\n",
           "Roll", "Name", "Sub1", "Sub2", "Sub3", "Average");
    printf("----------------------------------------------------------------------\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        float avg = (s[i].marks[0] + s[i].marks[1] + s[i].marks[2]) / 3.0f;
        printf("%-8d %-20s %8.2f %8.2f %8.2f %10.2f\n",
               s[i].roll, s[i].name,
               s[i].marks[0], s[i].marks[1], s[i].marks[2], avg);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Tabular Output (`n = 2`):**

```text
Roll     Name                     Sub1     Sub2     Sub3    Average
----------------------------------------------------------------------
101      Ananya Roy              84.00    90.00    87.00      87.00
102      Rohan Das               76.00    82.00    79.00      79.00
```

> [!MARKS] 3 + 7 = 10 Marks
> **a) Parameter Passing Concept (`3m`):** Actual vs formal parameters and stack activation transfer | **b) Types of Parameter Passing (`7m`):** Call by Value vs Call by Address (`[!COMPARE]` table) + complete `swap` C programs & stack traces.

### a) Meaning of Parameter Passing in C (3 Marks)

- **Definition:** **Parameter passing** is the communication mechanism through which a calling function transfers input data or memory addresses (**actual arguments**) into the local variables declared in a called function's header (**formal parameters**).
- **Actual vs Formal Parameters:**
  - **Actual arguments:** Expressions supplied at the call site, e.g., `swap(&x, &y)`.
  - **Formal parameters:** Local stack variables created inside the callee's activation frame, e.g., `void swap(int *a, int *b)`, initialized when the call begins and destroyed when the function returns.

### b) Types of Parameter Passing in C with Examples (7 Marks)

Strictly speaking, C evaluates every argument by value, yielding **two practical parameter-passing techniques**:

> [!COMPARE] Call by Value vs Call by Address (Reference) in C
> | Parameter | Call by Value | Call by Address (Pointer Passing) |
> |---|---|---|
> | **What Is Passed** | A **copy of the value** of each actual argument (`swap(x, y)`). | The **memory address** (`&x, &y`) of each actual variable. |
> | **Formal Parameter Type** | Ordinary variables (`int a, int b`). | Pointer variables (`int *a, int *b`). |
> | **Effect on Caller's Variables** | **Cannot modify** caller's original variables; changes stay local. | **Directly modifies** caller's variables via dereferencing `*a`, `*b`. |
> | **Memory & Copy Cost** | Duplicates data on the stack (costly for large `struct`s). | Copies only a fixed `4`/`8`-byte address (zero-copy for arrays/`struct`s). |
> | **Primary Use Case** | Pure mathematical functions (`sqrt(x)`, `max(a, b)`). | In-place `swap()`, array mutation, returning multiple outputs. |

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void swapByValue(int a, int b) {
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp; /* modifies local copies only */
}

void swapByAddress(int *a, int *b) {
    int temp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = temp; /* mutates caller variables through pointers */
}

int main(void) {
    int x = 10, y = 25;

    swapByValue(x, y);
    printf("After Call by Value:   x = %d, y = %d\n", x, y);

    swapByAddress(&x, &y);
    printf("After Call by Address: x = %d, y = %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Output & Trace:**
  - `After Call by Value:   x = 10, y = 25` (`x` and `y` remain unchanged because `a` and `b` occupy separate stack slots).
  - `After Call by Address: x = 25, y = 10` (`*a` and `*b` dereference `&x` and `&y`, swapping the caller's variables in place; note 1D arrays passed to functions also decay automatically to a pointer `&arr[0]`).

> [!MARKS] 3 + 7 = 10 Marks
> **a) `switch-case` Advantages (`3m`):** Jump-table dispatch, single evaluation, cleaner multi-way readability, and case grouping | **b) Dot-Separated Abbreviation Program (`7m`):** Complete C program converting `"SWAPAN KUMAR SEN"` -> `"S.K.S."` + dry-run trace.

### a) Advantages of `switch-case` Over Nested `if-else` (3 Marks)

- **1. `O(1)` Jump-Table Execution Speed:** When matching dense integer/character constants, compilers translate `switch` into an indexed **branch/jump table**, jumping directly to the matching `case` instead of testing `n` sequential `if-else` conditions.
- **2. Single Expression Evaluation:** `switch (expr)` evaluates the controlling expression **once**, avoiding repeated side effects or redundant calls across `else if` branches.
- **3. Superior Multi-Way Readability:** Flat `case` labels eliminate deep indentation pyramids in menu-driven programs.
- **4. Clean Multi-Value Grouping:** Adjacent labels (`case 'a': case 'e': case 'i':`) intentionally share one handler block without verbose `||` chains.
- **5. Compile-Time Duplicate Detection:** The C compiler immediately flags duplicate `case` constants as syntax errors (note: `switch` works only on integral/`char` constant expressions, not ranges or `float`s).

### b) C Program to Abbreviate a Multiword Name (`S.K.S.`) (7 Marks)

- **Word-Start Detection Logic:** A character `str[i]` begins a word whenever it is a non-whitespace character **and** either `i == 0` or the preceding character `str[i - 1]` is whitespace (`' '` or `'\t'`). This handles leading spaces and multiple consecutive spaces cleanly.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <ctype.h>

int main(void) {
    char str[120];

    printf("Enter a multiword string: ");
    if (fgets(str, sizeof(str), stdin) == NULL) {
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Abbreviated form: ");
    for (int i = 0; str[i] != '\0' && str[i] != '\n'; i++) {
        int isNonSpace = !isspace((unsigned char)str[i]);
        int atWordStart = (i == 0) || isspace((unsigned char)str[i - 1]);
        if (isNonSpace && atWordStart) {
            printf("%c.", toupper((unsigned char)str[i]));
        }
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

- **Dry-Run Trace on `"SWAPAN KUMAR SEN"`:**
  - `i = 0` (`'S'`, `i == 0`): word start -> prints **`S.`**
  - `i = 1..5` (`"WAPAN"`) & `i = 6` (`' '`): skipped
  - `i = 7` (`'K'`, preceded by `' '`): word start -> prints **`K.`**
  - `i = 8..11` (`"UMAR"`) & `i = 12` (`' '`): skipped
  - `i = 13` (`'S'`, preceded by `' '`): word start -> prints **`S.`**
  - **Final Output:** **`S.K.S.`**

