### a) Greatest & Smallest Element with Locations in a 2D Array (6 Marks)

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int rows, cols;
    int mat[20][20];

    printf("Enter rows and columns (1-20): ");
    if (scanf("%d %d", &rows, &cols) != 2 ||
        rows <= 0 || rows > 20 || cols <= 0 || cols > 20) {
        printf("Invalid dimensions.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Enter %d integers for the %d x %d matrix:\n", rows * cols, rows, cols);
    for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++) {
            scanf("%d", &mat[i][j]);
        }
    }

    int maxVal = mat[0][0], maxR = 0, maxC = 0;
    int minVal = mat[0][0], minR = 0, minC = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++) {
            if (mat[i][j] > maxVal) {
                maxVal = mat[i][j];
                maxR = i;
                maxC = j;
            }
            if (mat[i][j] < minVal) {
                minVal = mat[i][j];
                minR = i;
                minC = j;
            }
        }
    }

    printf("Greatest = %d at index [%d][%d] (Row %d, Col %d)\n",
           maxVal, maxR, maxC, maxR + 1, maxC + 1);
    printf("Smallest = %d at index [%d][%d] (Row %d, Col %d)\n",
           minVal, minR, minC, minR + 1, minC + 1);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Run (`2 × 3` matrix `{{14, 92, 7}, {35, 3, 61}}`):**
  - `Greatest = 92 at index [0][1] (Row 1, Col 2)`
  - `Smallest = 3 at index [1][1] (Row 2, Col 2)` (`O(rows * cols)` time, `O(1)` extra space).

### b) Pointer to a Function: Definition & Example (2 + 2 = 4 Marks)

- **Definition (`2m`):** A **pointer to a function** is a pointer variable that stores the **entry-point address of executable function code** rather than data. Syntax: `return_type (*fptr)(param_types);` (parentheses around `*fptr` are mandatory so `*` binds to `fptr` rather than the return type).
- **Runnable Example (`2m`):**

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int multiply(int a, int b) {
    return a * b;
}

int main(void) {
    int (*op)(int, int) = multiply; /* op holds entry address of multiply */
    printf("Product via function pointer = %d\n", op(6, 7)); /* prints 42 */
    return 0;
}
```

> [!MARKS] 3 + 7 = 10 Marks
> **a) C File-Opening Modes (`3m`):** Standard text and binary `fopen()` mode strings | **b) `NUMBER` -> `EVEN` / `ODD` File Program (`7m`):** Complete C program creating `NUMBER`, splitting integers by parity into `EVEN` and `ODD`, and verifying contents.

### a) File-Opening Modes in `fopen(filename, mode)` (3 Marks)

| Text Mode | Binary Equivalent | Allowed Operations | Behaviour if File Exists / Does Not Exist |
|---|---|---|---|
| **`"r"`** | `"rb"` | Read only | Opens at start; returns **`NULL`** if file does not exist. |
| **`"w"`** | `"wb"` | Write only | **Truncates** existing content to `0` bytes; creates file if absent. |
| **`"a"`** | `"ab"` | Append at end | Preserves existing data and writes at EOF; creates file if absent. |
| **`"r+"`** | `"rb+"` / `"r+b"` | Read + Write | Opens existing file without truncation; returns `NULL` if absent. |
| **`"w+"`** | `"wb+"` / `"w+b"` | Read + Write | Truncates existing file or creates a new empty file. |
| **`"a+"`** | `"ab+"` / `"a+b"` | Read + Append | Reads anywhere; all writes are forced to EOF (creates if absent). |

### b) C Program Splitting `NUMBER` into `EVEN` and `ODD` Files (7 Marks)

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    FILE *fNum, *fEven, *fOdd;
    int count, val;

    /* Step 1: Create NUMBER and store user-supplied integers */
    fNum = fopen("NUMBER", "w");
    if (fNum == NULL) {
        printf("Error creating NUMBER file.\n");
        return 1;
    }
    printf("How many integers to insert into NUMBER? ");
    if (scanf("%d", &count) != 1 || count <= 0) {
        fclose(fNum);
        return 1;
    }
    printf("Enter %d integers: ", count);
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        scanf("%d", &val);
        fprintf(fNum, "%d ", val);
    }
    fclose(fNum);

    /* Step 2: Reopen NUMBER for reading; open EVEN and ODD for writing */
    fNum  = fopen("NUMBER", "r");
    fEven = fopen("EVEN", "w");
    fOdd  = fopen("ODD", "w");
    if (fNum == NULL || fEven == NULL || fOdd == NULL) {
        printf("Error opening files for splitting.\n");
        if (fNum)  fclose(fNum);
        if (fEven) fclose(fEven);
        if (fOdd)  fclose(fOdd);
        return 1;
    }

    while (fscanf(fNum, "%d", &val) == 1) {
        if (val % 2 == 0) {
            fprintf(fEven, "%d ", val);
        } else {
            fprintf(fOdd, "%d ", val);
        }
    }

    fclose(fNum);
    fclose(fEven);
    fclose(fOdd);
    printf("Successfully split integers into EVEN and ODD files.\n");
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Trace:** Input `12 7 25 40 -6 19` written to `NUMBER` -> `EVEN` receives `12 40 -6 ` and `ODD` receives `7 25 19 `.

> [!MARKS] 4 + 6 = 10 Marks
> **a) Entry-Controlled vs Exit-Controlled Loops (`4m`):** Side-by-side comparison across 5 parameters | **b) Prime Numbers in `[lower, upper]` (`6m`):** Complete C program using `d * d <= n` trial division + sample run.

### a) Entry-Controlled vs Exit-Controlled Loops (4 Marks)

> [!COMPARE] Entry-Controlled Loop vs Exit-Controlled Loop in C
> | Parameter | Entry-Controlled Loop | Exit-Controlled Loop |
> |---|---|---|
> | **Condition Placement** | Continuation condition is tested **at the top (entry)** before the body. | Continuation condition is tested **at the bottom (exit)** after the body. |
> | **Minimum Body Runs** | **`0` times**—body is skipped completely if initial condition is false. | **`1` time**—body always executes once before the first test. |
> | **C Constructs** | **`for`** loop and **`while`** loop. | **`do-while`** loop (`do { ... } while (cond);`). |
> | **Header / Footer `;`** | No semicolon after `while (cond)` or `for (...)` header. | Ends with a **mandatory semicolon `;`** after `while (cond);`. |
> | **Typical Use Case** | Array traversal, file EOF reading, range iteration. | Interactive menu prompts and input-validation retries. |

### b) C Program to Print All Prime Numbers in a Given Range (6 Marks)

- **Primality Logic:** An integer `n >= 2` is prime iff no divisor `d` in `2 <= d <= sqrt(n)` (`d * d <= n`) divides `n` evenly (`n <= 1` are never prime).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int isPrime(int n) {
    if (n < 2) {
        return 0;
    }
    for (int d = 2; d * d <= n; d++) {
        if (n % d == 0) {
            return 0;
        }
    }
    return 1;
}

int main(void) {
    int lower, upper, count = 0;

    printf("Enter lower and upper range limits: ");
    if (scanf("%d %d", &lower, &upper) != 2) {
        return 1;
    }
    if (lower > upper) {
        int temp = lower;
        lower = upper;
        upper = temp;
    }

    printf("Prime numbers between %d and %d:\n", lower, upper);
    for (int num = lower; num <= upper; num++) {
        if (isPrime(num)) {
            printf("%d ", num);
            count++;
        }
    }
    printf("\nTotal primes found = %d\n", count);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Output (`lower = 10, upper = 30`):**
  - `Prime numbers between 10 and 30:` **`11 13 17 19 23 29`**
  - `Total primes found = 6`

> [!MARKS] 6 + 4 = 10 Marks
> **a) Cumulative Triangular Series Program (`6m`):** Complete C program reading `n` + step-by-step trace & closed-form verification | **b) Avoiding `goto` (`4m`):** Four structured-programming hazards + preferred C constructs.

### a) C Program for `S = 1 + (1+2) + (1+2+3) + ...` up to `n` Terms (6 Marks)

> [!FORMULA] Series Structure
> The `i`-th bracket equals the triangular sum `T_i = 1 + 2 + ... + i = i(i + 1) / 2`, and the `n`-term cumulative sum equals `S_n = n(n + 1)(n + 2) / 6`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    long totalSum = 0;

    printf("Enter number of terms (n): ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n <= 0) {
        printf("Please enter a positive integer.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        long bracketSum = 0;
        for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
            bracketSum += j;     /* computes (1 + 2 + ... + i) */
        }
        totalSum += bracketSum;  /* adds i-th bracket to grand total */
    }

    printf("Sum of the series up to %d terms = %ld\n", n, totalSum);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Dry-Run Trace (`n = 5`):**
  - `i = 1`: `bracketSum = 1` -> `totalSum = 1`
  - `i = 2`: `bracketSum = 3` -> `totalSum = 4`
  - `i = 3`: `bracketSum = 6` -> `totalSum = 10`
  - `i = 4`: `bracketSum = 10` -> `totalSum = 20`
  - `i = 5`: `bracketSum = 15` -> `totalSum =` **`35`** (`5 * 6 * 7 / 6 = 35`).

### b) Why We Should Avoid `goto` in C Programs (4 Marks)

- **1. Unstructured "Spaghetti" Control Flow:** Arbitrary forward and backward `goto` jumps destroy top-to-bottom sequential reading and violate the Bohm-Jacopini structured programming theorem.
- **2. Difficult Debugging & Maintenance:** When a `label:` is targeted from multiple scattered `goto` statements, tracking how variables reached their current state becomes error-prone.
- **3. Skipped Variable Initializations:** Jumping forward past a declaration like `int sum = 0;` leaves `sum` holding indeterminate stack garbage.
- **4. Resource Leak Risk & Poor Optimization:** Jumping out of blocks prematurely can skip `fclose()` or `free()` calls and hinders compiler loop optimizations.
- **Structured Replacements:** Express branching with `if-else` / `switch`, repetition with `for` / `while` / `do-while`, and early exits with `break`, `continue`, or modular `return` functions.

> [!MARKS] 2 + 3 + 5 = 10 Marks
> **a) Pointer Definition (`2m`):** Typed address variable + `&` and `*` | **b) Advantages Over Ordinary Variables (`3m`):** Five concrete advantages | **c) 1D Array via Pointer (`5m`):** Complete C program + pointer-scaling explanation.

### a) Pointer in C (2 Marks)

- **Definition:** A **pointer** is a derived variable that stores the **memory address** of another typed variable or heap block rather than holding a direct data value.
- **Declaration & Operators:** Declared as `data_type *ptr_name;` (e.g., `int x = 42; int *p = &x;`), where unary **`&`** fetches `x`'s byte address and unary **`*`** dereferences `p` to read/write `42`.

### b) Advantages of Pointer Variables Over Ordinary Memory Variables (3 Marks)

- **1. Call-by-Address Function Mutation:** Allows functions (`swap(&a, &b)`) to modify caller variables and return multiple computed results.
- **2. Dynamic Heap Memory Management:** Essential handle for runtime memory allocated via `malloc()`, `calloc()`, and `realloc()`.
- **3. Zero-Copy Passing of Arrays & Large `struct`s:** Passing an `8`-byte pointer avoids copying hundreds of bytes onto the stack.
- **4. Dynamic Linked Data Structures:** Enables self-referential nodes (`struct Node *next;`) required to build linked lists, trees, and graphs.
- **5. Efficient Sequential Traversal:** Advancing a pointer (`p++`) steps directly through contiguous array/string buffers.

### c) C Program to Access a 1D Array Using a Pointer (5 Marks)

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n, sum = 0;
    int arr[50];
    int *p = arr; /* arr decays to &arr[0] */

    printf("Enter number of elements (1-50): ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n <= 0 || n > 50) {
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Enter %d integers: ", n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        scanf("%d", p + i);       /* stores at address &arr[i] */
    }

    printf("\nIndex  Address         Value\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("arr[%d] %p  %d\n", i, (void *)(p + i), *(p + i));
        sum += *(p + i);
    }
    printf("Total sum = %d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Address Scaling Formula:** `p + i` computes byte address **`base_address + i * sizeof(int)`**, and `*(p + i)` is guaranteed by the C standard to be identical to `arr[i]`.

